distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efda95f0-5dcd-5aff-a3e2-001e3cc26a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284304} {:answer/id #uuid "8c47a968-2571-54a2-885a-ed45df9cd59c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284305} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8620b8-2e45-5c76-bbcd-a5670545d92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284306} {:answer/id #uuid "29bb6638-1e62-5371-a72e-5100891d3700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284307} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9fab5b-de8d-579a-b91a-92e2fa5dd08c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d9fab5b-de8d-579a-b91a-92e2fa5dd08c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284308} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7a7b06-862d-5083-86ca-fda7c303154e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35d12d0a-00b0-5153-a6da-b9f29bfa020a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130478" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $(CA)B=C(BA)$\n- $(A+A)B=2AB$\n- $CB=BC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $(CA)B=C(AB)$ is always true. However,  matrix multiplication is not commutative,   $AB\\ne BA$ in general. Therefore, $(CA)B\\ne C(BA)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+A)B=AB+AB=2AB$ is always true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, so $CB\\ne BC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}]}
